Better: make `IObserver`? Meh. Practical compromise: Orchestrator periodically (every ~1s when online) calls `RefreshAllState()` which the component wraps around state properties, OR orchestrator triggers `NotifyStateChanged()`-like via callbacks. Honestly, simplest robust pattern used widely: Component's CodeBehind owns the orchestrator as field, and orchestrator directly modifies the component's `State` properties (they're public; component instance is accessible from within same assembly — it's the same app domain!). In Blazor WASM, code behind and markup live in same assembly; modifying State from another class in the same assembly is technically allowed but the rendered DOM reflects the latest value on next render cycle — timing fine (State is re-evaluated on every render; rapid writes slightly lag but visually fine). HOWEVER — Blazor renders based on component instances; assigning State from another class may cause a render but could conflict if state mutated concurrently during interactivity? It's all single-threaded per component lifecycle; assignments are safe. I've seen this pattern (service ↔ component state bridging). But there's a subtlety: State derives from BaseState which supports `await` changes; synchronous assignment is fine too.

